\title{WSSmod: Weighted Sum of Scores for Protein Network Modules}
\description{
\if{html}{\figure{logo.png}{options: style='float: right' alt='logo' width='120'}}

Computes Weighted Sum of Scores (WSS) module-level summaries from proteomic data. Protein-level values are sign-aligned to their association with core biomarkers and combined within 'STRING' network modules, weighted by network connectivity and association strength. Bundled reference modules and a fitted 'joinet' model also allow all module scores to be predicted from age, sex and a small panel of plasma biomarkers, without measuring the full proteomic panel.
}
\details{
WSSmod computes Weighted Sum of Scores (WSS) module scores, and predicts
them from a small panel of plasma biomarkers.

\strong{Scoring modules from proteomic data}
\itemize{
\item \code{\link[=calculate_WSS]{calculate_WSS()}} scores each module from a normalized and scaled
samples-by-proteins matrix, using the bundled reference modules or your own.
\item \code{\link[=project_rank_norm]{project_rank_norm()}} projects new values onto a frozen rank-normal
reference, for normalizing new samples against a reference cohort.
}

\strong{Predicting module scores from plasma biomarkers}
\itemize{
\item \code{\link[=normalize_wss_biomarkers]{normalize_wss_biomarkers()}} rank-normalizes raw biomarker values, for a
single patient or a cohort.
\item \code{\link[=predict_WSS]{predict_WSS()}} predicts all module scores from age, sex and the
normalized biomarkers (needs the suggested packages \code{glmnet} and
\code{joinet}).
}

\strong{Bundled reference data}
\itemize{
\item \code{\link[=list_prebuilt_wss]{list_prebuilt_wss()}}, \code{\link[=load_prebuilt_wss]{load_prebuilt_wss()}}, \code{\link[=wss_prebuilt_terms]{wss_prebuilt_terms()}},
\code{\link[=load_prebuilt_wss_model]{load_prebuilt_wss_model()}} and \code{\link[=load_prebuilt_biomarker_reference]{load_prebuilt_biomarker_reference()}}
give access to the prebuilt reference set.
\item \link{wss_example_expression} and \link{wss_example_biomarkers} are small simulated
datasets for trying out the functions.
}
